  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1031 PM MDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nAs the Pacific Northwest trough tracks into Canada, the high \ncentered over the Southern Plains starts to drift back toward the \nwest. As a result, flow aloft shifts from southwesterly to more \nzonal Sunday, shifting the moisture plume north and east of the \nregion. The best moisture will remain pooled along the Divide, and \nlingering across northeast Utah and northwest Colorado. Instability \nwill be plentiful, provided morning cloud cover clears early enough. \nThe one thing that will be lacking is upper level support, as the \nshortwave from today will be well to the east. Instead, orographic \nforcing will be the primary trigger for afternoon convection, with \nshowers and storms firing off the terrain in the early afternoon. \nThis activity will move off the terrain and over adjacent valleys \nthrough the afternoon and evening, but will likely not be as long-\nlasting as convection this afternoon. Heavy rain remains a concern, \nas surface levels remain well saturated and mixing ratios of 5-7 \ng/kg remain. This will keep flash flood and debris flow concerns for \nsensitive areas, including recent burn scars. \n\nAbove normal moisture sticks around through the coming week, as the \nhigh gradually shifts back into the Desert Southwest. Forcing will \nbe somewhat lacking, with only the occasional shortwave rotating \naround the periphery of the high. This means that most afternoons \nwill see isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms develop \nover the higher terrain, with occasional periods of more focus \nconvection coinciding with a passing wave. So long as surface levels \nremain saturated, heavy rain and flash flooding will remain \nconcerns, particularly for sensitive areas. Additionally, gusty \noutflow winds and lightning will be daily threats. Temperatures will \nstart around 5 degrees above normal, but will gradually warm through \nthe week under the influence of the high. By the end of the coming \nworkweek, look for highs to be 5-10 degrees above normal.",
